# SGI GLU (Mesa standalone glu) at git tag glu-9.0.1 — our delta from pristine upstream.
#
# Upstream: https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/glu at tag glu-9.0.1
#           (a GIT TAG, not a release tarball — see WHY 9.0.1 below)
# Apply from dll/opengl/glu32/src/ with: patch -p1 < vendor-patches/glu32.patch
#
# 127 of the 194 vendored files appear here and that number is almost entirely noise.
# 122 of them differ ONLY by `#include` lines being commented out; allow that one
# substitution and exactly FIVE files carry real content changes. Read the five first.
#
# dll/opengl/glu32/include/ is NOT in this patch because it is byte-identical to
# upstream. The file sets match exactly in both directions: 194 files, no additions,
# no omissions, no upstream directory skipped.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FIVE REAL CHANGES
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   src/include/gluos.h
#       Comments out <windows.h> and <malloc.h>, adds <string.h> and
#       #define WIN32_NO_STATUS. A build shim for this tree's header layout, and not
#       reducible to a -D: the <string.h> is a real source addition, and you cannot
#       -D away an #include upstream emits unconditionally.
#
#   src/libnurbs/internals/trimregion.h
#       Adds `~TrimRegion( void ) {}` under `#if defined(__REACTOS__) && defined(__clang__)`
#       with a /* FIXME: Inspect */ comment. Upstream declares no destructor at all.
#       Still unexplained; the FIXME is upstream-of-us, i.e. ours, and unresolved.
#
#   src/libnurbs/internals/subdivider.cc
#   src/libnurbs/internals/quilt.cc
#       Pure reflow, no semantic change — each splits one of upstream's
#       `if (p) p->deleteMe(pool);  p = 0;` one-liners onto two lines. Note that this
#       is the C-preprocessor-classic misleading indentation ("the second statement is
#       not in the if"), and the reflow makes the existing behaviour READ correctly
#       without altering it. Both are in the compiled set.
#
#   src/libutil/mipmap.c
#       Gives gluScaleImage an explicit `GLAPI` prefix that the other six exported
#       functions in the same file do not have — in ours OR upstream. A single-symbol,
#       asymmetric, unexplained dllexport edit. WORTH A MAINTAINER'S SECOND LOOK; not
#       changed here because nothing in the tree explains why this one symbol needs it.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE OTHER 122 FILES: COMMENTED-OUT INCLUDES, AND WHY THEY ARE SAFE
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every one of these differs only by `#include "x.h"` / `#include <x.h>` lines being
# turned into `//#include ...`. Normalising that substitution away drops the differing
# count from 127 to 5, which is how the five above were isolated.
#
# DO NOT BELIEVE THE OLD EXPLANATION, which this registry carried until 2026-09-20:
# that the CMake build force-includes everything through precomp.h via
# `add_pch(glu32 precomp.h ...)`, so the includes are redundant. That is wrong twice
# over. add_pch is `if(PCH) target_precompile_headers(...) else() <nothing> endif()`
# and PCH is OFF by default (PCH:BOOL=OFF in every build dir here), so precomp.h is
# force-included NOWHERE in an ordinary build. And even with PCH on it would not cover
# the C files, because glu32's CMakeLists puts all of ${SOURCE} into PCH_SKIP_SOURCE.
#
# What is actually true, and it is an observation rather than a mechanism: glu32.dll
# builds and links with PCH off, so in this tree those headers arrive transitively
# through the includes that were left alive (subdivider.cc keeps subdivider.h, which
# reaches arc.h, bin.h, mapdesc.h and the rest). The edits were plausibly made for a
# PCH build and happen to also hold without one.
#
# CONSEQUENCE FOR A RE-VENDOR: this transform is pure liability and buys nothing in
# the default configuration. If you bump GLU, prefer dropping it and taking upstream's
# includes verbatim over reapplying it — then the tree is unmodified but for the five
# files above, and three of those are a shim, a FIXME and a reflow.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY 9.0.1, AND THE LICENCE SPLIT
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The drop carries no release stamp; include/GL/glu.h states GLU_VERSION_1_3, which is
# an API level, not a version of this source. 9.0.1 is established by file set and by
# content: we carry src/libtess/README and src/libtess/alg-outline, which exist only in
# the git tree and are excluded from `make dist`, and we do NOT carry src/meson.build,
# which 9.0.2 added. After normalising the include transform, differing files number 13
# against 9.0.0, FIVE against 9.0.1, 7 against 9.0.2, 8 against 9.0.3 and 18 against
# master. 9.0.1 is the unambiguous minimum.
#
# LICENCE IS NOT UNIFORM: 73 of the 194 files — the .cc under src/libnurbs/{interface,
# internals,nurbtess} — carry "SGI Free Software License B, Version 1.1", while the
# other 117 carry Version 2.0. That split is present in pristine glu-9.0.1, so it is
# inherited from Mesa and 0% ours. Three files carry no licence header at all
# (src/include/gluos.h, src/libtess/README, src/libtess/alg-outline) and all three are
# byte-for-byte that way upstream too — unlike adns's getopt.c, this is not something
# we stripped.
#
